steps.body:
Number the items from 0 and give each one a `parent` entry. At first every item is its own parent, so each item is a group of one. The item at the top of a group, the one that is its own parent, is the group's **root**, and it names the group.

```javascript
const parent = Array.from({ length: 5 }, (_, i) => i);
console.log(parent.join(" ")); // 0 1 2 3 4
```

`find(x)` follows parents up to the root. `union(a, b)` finds both roots and, if they differ, hangs one root under the other, which merges the two groups. Two items are connected exactly when `find` gives the same root for both.

```javascript harness
const parent = Array.from({ length: 5 }, (_, i) => i);
{{code}}
union(0, 1);
union(3, 4);
union(1, 4);
console.log(parent.join(" "), find(0) === find(3), find(2) === find(0));
```

```javascript
function find(x) {
  while (parent[x] !== x) {
    x = parent[x];
  }
  return x;
}

function union(a, b) {
  const rootA = find(a);
  const rootB = find(b);
  if (rootA !== rootB) {
    parent[rootB] = rootA;
  }
}
```

Always link roots. `parent[b] = a` on an item that isn't a root would cut it away from the rest of its group. Each union that merges two groups leaves one group fewer, so a counter that starts at n and drops by 1 on every real merge counts the connected groups.

**The gotcha:** compare roots, never parents. `parent[a] === parent[b]` can be false for two items in the same group, because one may sit deeper in the tree than the other.

