steps.body:
An edge between two items that are already in the same group closes a cycle: there was a path between them before this edge. `union` returns `false` exactly then, so finding the edge that closes a cycle is one loop:

```javascript harness
const parent = Array.from({ length: 5 }, (_, i) => i);
function find(x) {
  while (parent[x] !== x) {
    x = parent[x];
  }
  return x;
}
function union(a, b) {
  const rootA = find(a);
  const rootB = find(b);
  if (rootA === rootB) {
    return false;
  }
  parent[rootB] = rootA;
  return true;
}
{{code}}
```

```javascript
for (const [a, b] of [[1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4], [2, 4]]) {
  if (!union(a, b)) {
    console.log(`${a}-${b} closes a cycle`); // 2-4 closes a cycle
  }
}
```

The same check builds a **minimum spanning tree**, the cheapest set of edges that connects everything (Kruskal's algorithm). Sort the edges by weight, cheapest first, and keep each edge whose `union` succeeds. An edge that would close a cycle is skipped, since its two ends are already connected by cheaper edges. With n items, the tree is done after n - 1 kept edges, and fewer than that means some items can't be connected.

**The gotcha:** union find only tracks undirected connections. In a directed graph a cycle means following the arrows back to the start, which needs Kahn's algorithm or a DFS instead.

